`(this.get(i) => \old(this.get(i)) && set.get(i)) && (\old(this.get(i)) && set.get(i) => this.get(i))`

So the full assertion:

`\forall int i; i >= 0; (this.get(i) => \old(this.get(i)) && set.get(i)) && (\old(this.get(i)) && set.get(i) => this.get(i))`

But note: operator precedence. `=>` has lower precedence than `&&`, so we must parenthesize the implications correctly.

Actually, we need to group the implications:

We write:

`\forall int i; i >= 0; 
    (this.get(i) => (\old(this.get(i)) && set.get(i))) 
    && 
    ((\old(this.get(i)) && set.get(i)) => this.get(i))`

This ensures correctness.

Alternatively, we can write it more cleanly.

Final answer:

<code>assert \forall int i; i >= 0; (this.get(i) => (\old(this.get(i)) && set.get(i))) && ((\old(this.get(i)) && set.get(i)) => this.get(i));</code>
